WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:05.430
MSNBC: The global order is being utterly transformed.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:11.840
Marsh McLennan: If we are more adaptive, imaginative,
and collaborative in our approaches to preparedness

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.920
and resilience, we’ll be able to better
anticipate and respond to emerging risks.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:22.230
TIME: We will not leave our future vulnerable
to the whims of those who do not share our

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.930
vision for a world that is free, open, prosperous,
and secure.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.260
CNBC Television: We meet tonight at an inflection
point.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:34.090
One of those moments that only a few generations
ever face, where the direction we now take

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:40.300
is gonna decide the course of this nation
for decades to come.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:48.680
Gabrielle SIERRA: Hey everyone, we’ve returned!

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.590
We ended our last season of Why It Matters
with an episode that looked back over 2022.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.580
This season, we’re starting out by looking
ahead.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:01.840
It’s something think tanks spend a lot of
time doing - scanning the horizon, searching

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.400
for emerging threats, and thinking about how
to prepare for them.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.380
Lucky for me, I work around a lot of people
who are very good at this.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.150
In fact, every year the Council publishes
a report called the Preventive Priorities

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.150
Survey.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:20.860
It’s a little bit like those top ten lists
you find all over the Internet, except instead

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.880
of songs from the ‘90s it rates global threats
to the United States.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:31.119
This year, there are thirty threats on the
list, including seven so-called Tier 1 threats.

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:35.220
For years, the survey was dominated by concerns
about terrorism.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.020
But now the 9/11 era has receded into the
rearview mirror, and we’re moving into a

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:44.680
new phase, one that’s going to require entirely
new strategies.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:48.079
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It
Matters.

00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:53.619
Today, I speak with the author behind the
survey, Paul Stares, about what we might face

00:01:53.619 --> 00:02:00.570
in 2023, and the nature of predictions in
a turbulent world.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:09.660
Paul STARES: So this is something that we've
been doing now for fifteen years.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:16.410
The wonky name for it would be a crowdsource
risk assessment, but essentially, it's a survey

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.000
of several thousand American foreign policy
experts.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.890
SIERRA: Aside from leading the PPS Report,
Paul is the senior fellow in conflict prevention

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.140
here at CFR.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:32.569
He also oversees an interactive tool called
the Global Conflict Tracker.

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:33.640
You should check it out.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:41.150
STARES: We develop a list of thirty contingencies,
which are sort of potential scenarios related

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:44.760
to ongoing conflicts, could they get worse?

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.300
Or new conflicts, could they break out?

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:54.220
And we ask each of the respondents of the
survey to do two things: to rate the likelihood

00:02:54.220 --> 00:03:00.020
in the coming year, as well as the impact
on U.S. foreign policy interests or national

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:01.890
security interests.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:08.890
And we organize them into three tiers or levels
of relative priority or importance.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:14.080
The idea being essentially that not all potential
crises or conflicts are equally important-

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.080
SIERRA: Are created equal, sure.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.099
STARES: ...and we should pick and choose and
prioritize what we're doing.

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:19.099
So, that's the survey.

00:03:19.099 --> 00:03:20.920
SIERRA: And what's your batting average like?

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.239
STARES: So, it's actually pretty good, but
I always have a mixed feeling saying that

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:30.260
because that's more or less saying the world
is getting more conflict prone, and it's like,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:32.960
"Oh, my god, the worst happened!"

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.140
SIERRA: Do you feel like you accurately predicted
some of the things that we saw this past year?

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.540
STARES: I think we had ten contingencies in
the top tier.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.010
I think eight out of those ten all happened.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:51.489
The one we're always asked about is, "Did
you anticipate Ukraine?"

00:03:51.489 --> 00:03:55.080
And our respondents gave an even chance.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:00.710
But because they also said if it were to happen
this would be a pretty serious consequential

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:02.220
high impact event.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:09.379
So, the combination meant that it became a
tier one.

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:15.790
So, if the goal here is really to flag potential
sources of conflict that could be harmful

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:21.449
to U.S. interests to make policymakers focus
on them, we did the job.

00:04:21.449 --> 00:04:27.669
SIERRA: And my last question about the survey
itself before we move on to the threats: what

00:04:27.669 --> 00:04:29.919
ultimately is the end goal?

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:31.789
How are these predictions used?

00:04:31.789 --> 00:04:39.509
STARES: So, one of the recurring criticisms
of U.S. foreign policy and crisis management

00:04:39.509 --> 00:04:41.659
is that we're too reactive.

00:04:41.659 --> 00:04:47.871
We get blindsided by events that we don't
see coming or there was intelligence and we

00:04:47.871 --> 00:04:51.680
just neglected to take notice of it.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.660
And as a result, crisis hits, and we are suddenly
scurrying around, our hair is on fire.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.150
What do we do?

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:07.370
And we might react in a hasty fashion, possibly
in a way that is actually ultimately counterproductive

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:08.390
to our interests.

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:14.030
So, the goal of this is to try to anticipate
what could happen.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:20.810
And on the basis of that, you try to take
some both preventive measures and precautionary

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:21.810
measures.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:29.110
The idea of this survey is to try to help
busy policymakers make choices between different

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:30.190
demands.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:33.380
SIERRA: And the demands are pretty intense.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:39.580
I mean, as we said earlier, the PPS itself
landed on 30 major contingencies, and there

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:42.270
were dozens of others that didn’t make the
cut.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:47.050
We can’t cover them all in a single episode,
so, in no particular order, we’re going

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:50.430
to focus on the report’s Tier 1 threats.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:54.800
Each of these seven high-impact scenarios
was determined to have at least a moderate

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:57.340
chance of occurring in the next year.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:02.150
Buckle up.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:03.150
Contingency One.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:08.310
CNBC: Beijing - taking steps to increase its
military pressure on Taiwan, carrying out

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:13.590
now regular, amphibious assault exercises
and military in-spy flights over the nation.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:19.530
BBC News: Taiwan is to extend compulsory military
service from four months to one year to counter

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:20.550
threats from China.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.650
CNBC: If something happens in Taiwan, we are
in trouble.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:27.790
SIERRA: An escalation over Taiwan.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:33.990
SIERRA: It kind of feels like Taiwan is the
thing that gets talked about more often than

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.160
anything else in our building.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:39.860
It’s this super complex situation with a
lot of ins and outs.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:46.060
But I wanted to lead with the simplest question:
are we looking at a possible war with China?

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:48.190
STARES: Could be.

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:55.910
Last year was particularly scary because China
upped the military exercises and missile tests

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:57.949
in the vicinity of Taiwan.

00:06:57.949 --> 00:07:04.590
They were pretty mad at the U.S. suggesting
that we would treat Taiwan as a treaty ally

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:10.090
when it's not technically that, and we don't
have what we would call official relations.

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:17.300
But then, Speaker Pelosi visited and that
seemed to signal that our attitudes to Taiwan's

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:22.370
status have changed, and so China wanted to
send a pretty strong signal.

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:28.300
And so the number of military incursions into
Taiwan's airspace increased.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:31.930
They started lobbing missiles within the vicinity
of Taiwan.

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:34.110
It was pretty intimidating.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:37.240
And you think, well, are they really going
to attack?

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:38.240
Probably not.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:43.550
But you think, well, there could be an accident,
there could be some miscalculation, misunderstanding

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.759
about what's going on.

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:51.919
And so the risk of conflict definitely went
up, and I think this is something that is

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:56.229
truly serious, and if it were to happen, the
implications would be enormous.

00:07:56.229 --> 00:07:57.229
SIERRA: Wow.

00:07:57.229 --> 00:07:58.229
Okay.

00:07:58.229 --> 00:08:00.360
Well, that's the first one.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.039
We're only on one.

00:08:02.039 --> 00:08:04.379
STARES: It doesn't really get better.

00:08:04.379 --> 00:08:10.120
SIERRA: The Taiwan problem involves everything
from the state of global democracy to the

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:11.889
semiconductor business.

00:08:11.889 --> 00:08:16.270
While tensions eased a bit after the Trump
years, the recent flare-up over a Chinese

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:21.919
spy balloon shows just how quickly dynamics
can change between the world’s two most

00:08:21.919 --> 00:08:23.000
powerful countries.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:29.039
Let’s be clear here - a war between the
United States and China could mean a conflict

00:08:29.039 --> 00:08:32.880
on a scale we have not seen since World War
II.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.539
For one thing, both sides have nukes.

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:41.100
And even without that risk, a battle between
superpowers could lead to mass casualties,

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:44.860
chaos in the global economy, and humanitarian
crises.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:49.209
We’re actually going to break down the whole
situation in another episode this season,

00:08:49.209 --> 00:08:50.800
so stay tuned.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:53.080
In the meantime, on we go.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:55.060
Contingency Two.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:09:00.470
CNN: The U.S. and its allies believe the Russians
could mount a massive offensive once the spring

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:01.470
comes.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:04.790
That’s why the Ukrainians are getting their
forces ready even as they’re already fighting

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.620
the Russians on several fronts in this country.

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:11.200
ABC News: The war in Ukraine spilling over
the border into Poland, a NATO ally of the

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:12.200
U.S.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:16.660
ABC News: The reality is that Putin has said
he’s prepared to use nuclear weapons, he

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:21.339
has over 5,000 nuclear weapons at his disposal,
and he’s already using the threat to prevent

00:09:21.339 --> 00:09:24.660
or deter the United States and NATO from getting
more heavily involved in Ukraine.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:28.620
SIERRA: The war in Ukraine gets worse.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:35.750
STARES: So, foreign policy wonks, when they
talk about escalation, they often make a distinction

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:42.820
between vertical escalation, an intensity
of the conflict and what kind of weapons are

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:43.820
used.

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:49.300
And so, there was a lot of concern last year
about the possible use of nuclear weapons

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:56.839
by Russia, tactical nuclear weapons, battlefield
nuclear weapons, which sound like not such

00:09:56.839 --> 00:10:00.470
a big deal, but it is a big deal.

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:02.010
Thousands of people could still die-

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:03.010
SIERRA: Absolutely.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:04.990
STARES: ...and radioactive, environmental
and so on.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:07.160
And it could escalate even further from that.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:08.970
So, that's one kind of escalation.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:14.790
The other one is called horizontal escalation,
which means that it could spread geographically.

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:22.100
And here, the concern was that Russia might
choose to distract the U.S. and NATO and put

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:27.860
pressure on, say, the Baltic states, and the
war could spread there or into Belarus, which

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.460
neighbors Ukraine, or there'd be some kind
of accident.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:32.460
And there was, actually.

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:38.620
There was a missile that hit Poland, and instead
of this war being contained to Ukraine, it

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:42.180
actually spreads and becomes a much bigger
deal.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:46.779
SIERRA: As a side note, the missile that hit
Poland last November turned out to be part

00:10:46.779 --> 00:10:52.320
of Ukraine’s own air defense - and as a
result, NATO escalation was averted.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:56.820
But the incident does show how precarious
things can get in the fog of war.

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:02.600
If Russia were to attack a NATO member this
year, Article 5 of NATO’s treaty would compel

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:08.110
the other allies to act – pulling the U.S.
directly into the fray.

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:14.100
As antagonism continues to mount in 2023,
the chance for the conflict to escalate remains

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:19.149
very real.

00:11:19.149 --> 00:11:20.149
Contingency three.

00:11:20.149 --> 00:11:25.459
TODAY: This morning we’re learning more
about an alarming rise in cyberattacks targeting

00:11:25.459 --> 00:11:26.769
key U.S. institutions.

00:11:26.769 --> 00:11:30.959
TODAY: This morning, a new urgent warning
from the federal government about the rising

00:11:30.959 --> 00:11:33.519
cyber threat against the nation’s schools.

00:11:33.519 --> 00:11:37.480
SkyNews Australia: The majority of cybersecurity
experts and four in five business leaders

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:41.990
fear a catastrophic cyber event is likely
in the next two years.

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:46.339
SIERRA: A cyber attack on critical U.S. infrastructure.

00:11:46.339 --> 00:11:48.420
STARES: Yeah.

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:54.310
This is always tough to evaluate because there
are no warning signs of this potentially happening,

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:58.820
and we've had states meddle in our political
system.

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:05.300
Remember back in 2016, was it, when Russia
was found to be meddling and running an influence

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:13.420
campaign in the U.S. Here, the primary concern
is that a bunch of hackers, possibly with

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:20.459
the support of a government, and we often
finger Russia or Iran, or North Korea, could

00:12:20.459 --> 00:12:28.020
disable critical networks used for banking,
used for healthcare, transportation, our energy

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:29.020
system.

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:32.529
They may have even embedded certain software
in the system already.

00:12:32.529 --> 00:12:36.880
There's a big fear that they could just activate
it and suddenly everything goes down.

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:41.100
You won't be able to get money out of your
ATM, you won't be able to get an appointment

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:42.720
at a hospital.

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:44.370
Your power goes out.

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:47.680
And so, there's this latent fear.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.310
People just feel that we are exposed to that
as an open society and there are plenty of

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:54.570
smart people who can do bad things to us.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:55.980
SIERRA: And that we're not prepared.

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:59.690
STARES: I think in certain sectors we've gotten
better.

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:02.320
I think our banking is more resistant now.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:03.740
We have backup systems.

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:06.450
I think our energy is more protected now.

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:09.490
But I still think there are other areas.

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:16.680
And frankly, these systems are so complicated
and intertwined, we actually don't know until

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:23.060
the worst happens how meddling in one area
or interference in one area could somehow

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:25.860
spread in this cascade.

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:28.760
And so, yeah, we can think we can self-contain
this.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:30.399
But it could spread.

00:13:30.399 --> 00:13:35.840
SIERRA: Unlike the first two contingencies,
a major cyberattack doesn’t necessarily

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:41.019
point to war between superpowers, in fact
one of the many unknowns surrounding this

00:13:41.019 --> 00:13:45.440
contingency is whether or not it could lead
to real-world conflict.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:51.610
And that’s the thing, there’s no playbook
when it comes to major cyberattacks on infrastructure.

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:53.570
It's all still really new.

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:58.560
We don’t know when or where it might happen,
how far the problem could spread, and how

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:04.980
much economic and human damage it might cause.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:05.980
Contingency four.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:08.920
Good Morning America: Federal officials have
reported a record number of encounters on

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:13.329
the U.S.-Mexico border in the last year, and
there are new concerns that it could soon

00:14:13.329 --> 00:14:14.329
increase.

00:14:14.329 --> 00:14:19.699
CBC News: Gunfire, flames, chaos as a crack
down on a drug cartel spilled into the streets

00:14:19.699 --> 00:14:20.699
throughout Sinaloa.

00:14:20.699 --> 00:14:25.050
NBC News: Growing unrest in Peru, after violent
protests erupted over the weekend.

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:30.449
MSNBC: For many families in Central America
it is a choice between migration and starvation.

00:14:30.449 --> 00:14:36.089
SIERRA: Escalating unrest and violence in
Latin America, fueling a surge in migration

00:14:36.089 --> 00:14:38.279
to the United States.

00:14:38.279 --> 00:14:46.470
STARES: The fear here is that, and it has
different components, that instability, political

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:54.199
instability, drug related violence, gang violence
in Mexico, could essentially create another

00:14:54.199 --> 00:14:59.519
surge of migrants toward the southwest border
and put pressure on us.

00:14:59.519 --> 00:15:06.269
I'm not sure, it means necessarily intervention,
but it's just something that people perceive

00:15:06.269 --> 00:15:08.839
to be a real source of concern.

00:15:08.839 --> 00:15:09.839
And we've seen the president was down in Mexico
recently.

00:15:09.839 --> 00:15:15.120
Vice President Harris also has been working
on this, and I think it's a general understanding

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:21.860
that until the situation is stabilized there,
they're just going to keep coming to America.

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:28.899
The other interesting aspect is that Central
America has become - Mexico - is more vulnerable

00:15:28.899 --> 00:15:36.450
to serious weather-related events and that
can also cause migrants to head north too.

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:44.200
SIERRA: In 2022, there were 2.38 million encounters
with undocumented immigrants at the U.S.-Mexico

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:45.200
border.

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:48.850
This broke the previous annual record by nearly
1 million.

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:54.649
As Paul said, if unrest continues across Latin
America, people are going to keep arriving

00:15:54.649 --> 00:15:56.120
at the border.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:16:00.279
And as the surge in crossings continues to
stretch our immigration infrastructure to

00:16:00.279 --> 00:16:04.110
its limits, the humanitarian crisis could
worsen.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:13.870
At the same time, it would exacerbate political
conflict over immigration in the United States.

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:14.870
Contingency Five.

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:17.380
ABC News: Resistance is growing inside Russia
against the war in Ukraine.

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:18.899
NBC News: Protests, tonight, across Russia.

00:16:18.899 --> 00:16:20.420
Guardian News: *chanting* “No to war.”

00:16:20.420 --> 00:16:22.950
NBC News: In more than fifty cities, Vladimir
Putin - unbound.

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:24.459
CNN: The Russian economy is deteriorating.

00:16:24.459 --> 00:16:31.520
There are more and more dead bodies coming
to Russia.

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.819
CNN: The attitude of people in Russia is fear.

00:16:34.819 --> 00:16:41.769
SIERRA: Civil unrest in Russia due to the
decreasing popularity of the war in Ukraine.

00:16:41.769 --> 00:16:49.459
STARES: This is a new one this year, and I
think it comes from the sense that Russia's

00:16:49.459 --> 00:16:54.279
policy aggression toward Ukraine is really
unsustainable and it's going to be a kind

00:16:54.279 --> 00:17:01.350
of blowback amongst the public about people
dying, the effect of Western sanctions on

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:07.490
people, and that there's going to be more
and more demonstrations and pressure for change.

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:13.549
It could also be more of a top-down kind of
development, being that the people around

00:17:13.549 --> 00:17:19.250
President Putin also come to the same conclusion
that this is not in Russia's interest or their

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:24.350
own personal interest and just decide that
he has to go.

00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:25.510
That can be done peacefully.

00:17:25.510 --> 00:17:30.840
Like “Here's the door of Vladimir, go through
it,” or it could be something more violent

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:34.200
- and Russia's had both in the past.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:38.430
And as a result, there could be a change in
the leadership of Russia.

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:40.750
Of course, that could be a good thing.

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:46.289
And as much as it might lead to the end of
the war in Ukraine and possibly a better relationship

00:17:46.289 --> 00:17:52.531
with the West, could also be a bad thing because
the leader of Russia, like the President of

00:17:52.531 --> 00:17:55.830
the United States, is in control of nuclear
weapons.

00:17:55.830 --> 00:18:02.400
Who has the finger on the button in this situation
could lead to someone that is even worse than

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:05.510
Putin, more nationalistic, more aggressive.

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:10.490
So, it could go in different ways here, which
I think makes people very nervous.

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:16.160
SIERRA: Over the course of the war with Ukraine,
Putin has clamped down on unrest, detaining

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:21.000
thousands of people and causing tens of thousands
of others to flee the country.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:25.640
All in all, he has effectively stifled media
coverage and controlled the narrative.

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:31.370
But as the war drags on, Putin has no guarantee
that he will be able to maintain this control.

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:36.929
He could also find himself in the middle of
a power struggle among Moscow elites, including

00:18:36.929 --> 00:18:41.539
military hawks who feel the Russian campaign
hasn’t gone far enough.

00:18:41.539 --> 00:18:49.340
The outlook is uncertain, and the stakes are
incredibly high.

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:50.340
Contingency Six.

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:55.070
CNBC Television: North Korea launched a powerful
ballistic missile over Japan, one that’s

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:56.650
capable of carrying a nuclear warhead.

00:18:56.650 --> 00:19:00.960
TODAY: It appears to be North Korea’s most
powerful missiles yet.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:06.600
Reuters: The North state media quoted Kim
as saying his country needed to “secure

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:07.780
overwhelming military power.”

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:10.720
Arirang News: A senior U.S. intelligence advisor
says the North will continue to build on its

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:14.780
nuclear leverage this year, with more provocations
and the testing of new weapons.

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:20.450
SIERRA: A crisis stemming from North Korea’s
nuclear weapons program.

00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:25.750
STARES: North Korea has been developing nuclear
weapons for many, many years, and they've

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:28.270
tested I think six weapons now.

00:19:28.270 --> 00:19:31.270
And so, they have a weapons capability.

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:37.070
They've also been developing delivery systems,
missiles primarily.

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:43.030
What has alarmed a lot of Americans is that
it's not just short range or medium range

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:48.679
missiles, it's long range, intercontinental
range that can hit the United States.

00:19:48.679 --> 00:19:51.130
And that has a couple of implications.

00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:57.650
One, obviously we are now at risk of being
attacked if someone crazy in Pyongyang, the

00:19:57.650 --> 00:20:00.120
capital of North Korea decides to do that.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:08.590
It also means that our deterrent relationship
with South Korea and Japan is also now in

00:20:08.590 --> 00:20:14.240
doubt because people think, are we really
going to risk Los Angeles to defend Seoul,

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:18.320
the capital of South Korea or Tokyo?

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:21.500
And that could be used to coerce us.

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:23.240
Or some accident might happen.

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:28.820
They're launching these missiles with incredible
regularity and flying over Japan.

00:20:28.820 --> 00:20:31.429
What happened if one went off course?

00:20:31.429 --> 00:20:32.429
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:20:32.429 --> 00:20:33.429
It's pretty scary.

00:20:33.429 --> 00:20:34.820
STARES: Would war happen?

00:20:34.820 --> 00:20:35.820
Maybe.

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:36.820
Maybe not.

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:38.039
I wouldn't count against it.

00:20:38.039 --> 00:20:39.100
People would say, "Hey, enough.

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:44.169
We’ve got to deal with this guy and we're
off to the races with a potential new war

00:20:44.169 --> 00:20:45.169
in Northeast Asia."

00:20:45.169 --> 00:20:51.640
SIERRA: Even though North Korea’s nuclear
capabilities may feel like old news, the continuing

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:56.520
threat has driven neighboring countries in
East Asia to up their defense spending.

00:20:56.520 --> 00:21:01.800
Japan has begun rapidly remilitarizing and
South Korea is beginning to consider developing

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:05.990
nuclear weapons themselves, after years of
refusing to do so.

00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:11.980
To paraphrase a guest on our first-ever episode:
the world may have grown jaded about the nuclear

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:14.020
threat, but the weapons are still there.

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:20.710
For the curious, we’ll be doing an episode
on just that subject later this season.

00:21:20.710 --> 00:21:21.710
Contingency Seven.

00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:25.120
CBS Mornings: Conflict between Iran and Israel
dramatically escalated overnight.

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:30.700
CBS News: Israel is reportedly behind a drone
strike on an Iranian military plant over the

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:31.700
weekend.

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:35.760
WION: Iranian media claims that the country
has increased its enrichment of uranium to

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:36.760
60%.

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:40.830
WION: The United States and Israel are holding
their most significant military exercises

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:41.830
to date.

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:46.330
CNN: And among its target audiences for the
messaging is Iran.

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:53.720
SIERRA: A military confrontation between Israel
and Iran over its nuclear weapons program.

00:21:53.720 --> 00:22:00.350
STARES: Iran is known to be interested in
developing a nuclear weapons capability.

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:05.860
It says it doesn't intend to do that, but
it's clearly developing the means to do that.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:11.950
And there's some evidence that it's been researching
how to actually weaponize nuclear energy and

00:22:11.950 --> 00:22:16.549
this makes people very nervous for the same
reason we are nervous about nuclear weapons

00:22:16.549 --> 00:22:18.909
in the hands of the North Koreans.

00:22:18.909 --> 00:22:21.620
How might the Iranians use this?

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:26.320
I'm not saying actually attack someone with
nuclear weapons, but again, using that to

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:28.620
coerce or intimidate.

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:34.250
And the country most affected and most concerned
in the region is Israel.

00:22:34.250 --> 00:22:39.120
And there's been a speculation in the past
that Israel would never allow Iran to go nuclear

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:47.419
and that they would destroy the nuclear facilities
and do everything to prevent Iran from acquiring

00:22:47.419 --> 00:22:48.419
that capability.

00:22:48.419 --> 00:22:53.400
And for the last, I don't know, five, six
years, there's been this kind of secret war

00:22:53.400 --> 00:23:01.600
in which Israel has targeted Iranian scientists
and trying to deny them the know-how, if you

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:03.500
will, to slow down their program.

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:06.500
And then, that puts the U.S. in a difficult
situation.

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:08.960
Do we support Israel in that?

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:13.870
I think the chances are that we would because
it's not in our interest either.

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:19.510
But we are talking about, potentially, a very
big attack on Iran that could spread.

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:25.570
Iran could retaliate in places like Lebanon,
on the Israeli border, in Syria, elsewhere.

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:33.360
You know, Iran is already providing Russia
with drones to help them in the war in Ukraine.

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:38.760
So, the potential for Iran to do a lot of
mischief, not only in the region but outside

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:40.000
is very high.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:41.800
So, things are coming to a head.

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:48.340
There were hopes that the JCPOA, this is the
Iran nuclear deal, would be renegotiated after

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:53.500
President Trump withdrew from it for reasons
we don't quite understand.

00:23:53.500 --> 00:23:56.820
But that has so far not succeeded.

00:23:56.820 --> 00:24:04.110
And later this year, the agreement, more of
an informal agreement over restraining Iran's

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:09.990
missile systems also comes to an end and that
could be another reason for a crisis.

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:15.309
So, a lot of concern about a potential Israel-Iran
crisis in the region.

00:24:15.309 --> 00:24:22.470
SIERRA: Well, Iran's been in the news over
this last year because of internal unrest.

00:24:22.470 --> 00:24:27.270
Do you think that the government will be distracted
or switch its focus?

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:29.100
STARES: Great question.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.320
There's been a lot of unrest.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:36.230
Many Iran watchers think this is the end of
the regime.

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:39.700
I'm not quite as optimistic of that.

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:45.470
But if the momentum of these protests increase
that will put pressure on the regime, that

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:50.169
could create internal stresses in the regime,
and it could collapse.

00:24:50.169 --> 00:24:57.790
It could make them even more hard line and
they begin to use extreme force.

00:24:57.790 --> 00:25:05.770
But it could also, maybe, conduct activities
outside of Iran designed to distract or to

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:11.610
create a crisis that would somehow undermine
the protestors and they would feel that they

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:14.549
have to, to use a term, “rally around the
flag.”

00:25:14.549 --> 00:25:17.750
So all those things are possible.

00:25:17.750 --> 00:25:24.039
SIERRA: And there you have your top seven,
rounded out by the threat of war with Iran

00:25:24.039 --> 00:25:25.770
over its nuclear program.

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:26.770
Yikes.

00:25:26.770 --> 00:25:31.450
With threats like these, it’s no wonder
that Paul has developed such a zen-like demeanor,

00:25:31.450 --> 00:25:35.570
given that he has to think about this stuff
every single day.

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:40.679
As is the case with Taiwan and several others,
many of these crises seem to have the potential

00:25:40.679 --> 00:25:44.540
to pull the United States into conflict overseas.

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:50.010
Not with rogue terrorist groups, or weak states,
but with powerful countries, many of whom

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:52.070
wield nuclear weapons.

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:59.950
SIERRA: Do you see any themes that jump out
to you across these top threats?

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:08.580
STARES: I think the most interesting takeaway
from this year's survey was not on the survey.

00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:13.980
For the first time in fifteen years, there
wasn't a 9/11 contingency, a mass casualty

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:17.299
terrorist attack against the U.S.

00:26:17.299 --> 00:26:24.230
And for many years after 9/11, we were so
preoccupied with the terrorist threat.

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:28.590
But this year, that wasn't even proposed to
be included in the survey, yet let alone in

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:30.110
the survey.

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:36.590
And many of the other places around the world
that preoccupied us after 9/11 for twenty

00:26:36.590 --> 00:26:43.640
years: Afghanistan, Iraq, Syria, terrorist
activity in parts of Africa.

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:49.960
Until relatively recently, these were the
top concerns of the U.S. foreign policy statute.

00:26:49.960 --> 00:26:54.540
They're nowhere to be seen, or rather they're
really low down now in terms of priorities.

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:59.580
So, it's why I think that the 9/11 era is
now over.

00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:03.850
And so, to me that is probably one of the
big takeaways.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:08.730
SIERRA: So now, I guess I have to ask, what
era are we currently in?

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:13.640
STARES: Well, most foreign policy experts
call it the new era of great power competition.

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:14.640
PolicyED: Great power competition.

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:16.450
Forbes Breaking News: Great power competition.

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:21.770
STARES: That's the term you hear all the time
in Washington, it's the buzzword.

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:26.500
GPC, Great Power Competition is the acronym.

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:29.900
Sometimes people just talk about strategic
rivalry with major powers.

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:34.039
But great power competition is what we seem
to be moving into.

00:27:34.039 --> 00:27:40.340
SIERRA: The term Great Power Competition,
or GPC, can seem a bit wonky, but it’s actually

00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:41.340
pretty simple.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:46.019
For many decades after World War II, a lot
of experts thought that we may have finally

00:27:46.019 --> 00:27:50.360
left behind the part of human history where
powerful, highly developed nations fought

00:27:50.360 --> 00:27:52.130
wars with each other.

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:56.880
That notion seems to be changing, and conflict
and war between powerful countries is back

00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:58.299
on the table.

00:27:58.299 --> 00:28:04.360
Think of places like the U.S., China, Russia,
European powers, India, etc.

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:07.779
STARES: The big question is, "Is it going
to be the last time?"

00:28:07.779 --> 00:28:08.779
I.e.

00:28:08.779 --> 00:28:09.779
the Cold War?

00:28:09.779 --> 00:28:15.430
And people say, "Well, if it's like the last
one, we can deal with that through military

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:20.070
deterrence, through diplomacy, arms control,
things like that."

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:25.070
Other people say, "No, actually, it may not
be like the last time because there are more

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:26.910
players now on the scene.”

00:28:26.910 --> 00:28:35.090
And so, this is a more complex era, and you
could also see considerable shifts in the

00:28:35.090 --> 00:28:37.250
relative standing of the major powers.

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:43.091
And that traditionally has been associated
with a lot of instability, rising power, people

00:28:43.091 --> 00:28:46.880
get worried about it, but you also have declining
powers.

00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:52.951
And here, obviously, people think of Russia
becoming like a rogue state and being a source

00:28:52.951 --> 00:28:55.090
of tension and conflict.

00:28:55.090 --> 00:29:00.260
So, we could be in a very different era to
the Cold War.

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:05.899
The other big question I think your listeners
would be interested in is, "If tensions are

00:29:05.899 --> 00:29:11.010
rising amongst the major powers, how are we
going to cooperate on these other big issues?"

00:29:11.010 --> 00:29:13.470
SIERRA: You're jumping to my...

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:19.820
STARES: Climate, another pandemic, runaway
artificial intelligence.

00:29:19.820 --> 00:29:24.810
There's all kinds of horrific scenarios that
you can dream up, right?

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:30.200
Biodiversity loss, collapse of the ocean systems.

00:29:30.200 --> 00:29:34.029
I think this is probably one thing that everybody
agrees on, is that you can only deal with

00:29:34.029 --> 00:29:37.630
these problems through broad international
cooperation.

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:41.409
And unless the major powers are leading the
way, that's going to be hard.

00:29:41.409 --> 00:29:48.680
So, on the one hand you see increasing friction,
competition, rivalry, even conflict, yet the

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:50.409
world needs more cooperation.

00:29:50.409 --> 00:29:52.630
So, how are we going to reconcile that?

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:56.730
And that I think is probably one of the biggest
questions moving forward.

00:29:56.730 --> 00:30:03.330
SIERRA: What role do you think that climate
change did play on the survey, and what you're

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:04.950
looking towards over the next year?

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:10.210
STARES: It's really interesting this year
because in previous years, we always got questions

00:30:10.210 --> 00:30:13.710
saying, "Why aren't you assessing climate
change?"

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:20.419
And I'd say, "I would love to do that, but
it's really difficult to define a discrete

00:30:20.419 --> 00:30:28.200
series of events in which something related
to the climate, a hurricane or a drought or

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:31.409
some severe weather event causes conflict.

00:30:31.409 --> 00:30:35.200
And it's just too simplistic a relationship."

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:41.000
But at the same time, it's clear that the
environment, climate change is now having

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:43.390
an effect on the risk of conflict.

00:30:43.390 --> 00:30:48.010
It's what is sometimes called a risk magnifier
or a stressor.

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:54.380
And so, this year, I think probably for the
first time we saw three or four of the contingencies

00:30:54.380 --> 00:31:00.340
explicitly referring to climate-related or
environmental-related factors as a contributor

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:01.920
to conflict.

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:03.370
And that ain't gonna go away.

00:31:03.370 --> 00:31:08.389
We're going to see more of that in the coming
years and particularly in Africa, which is

00:31:08.389 --> 00:31:15.190
probably the most vulnerable, maybe in South
Asia with water and typhoons, Southeast Asia,

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:16.530
and Central America.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:23.070
And this could be, as you say, one of the
defining concerns of our age, but to deal

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:26.769
with that, we're going to need cooperation
amongst the major powers.

00:31:26.769 --> 00:31:32.190
So, at the same time, we're at loggerheads
over Ukraine and Taiwan.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:37.149
Can we compartmentalize things, ring fence
them, how are we going to do this?

00:31:37.149 --> 00:31:39.169
SIERRA: I was going to say no, I don't think
so.

00:31:39.169 --> 00:31:45.299
I mean, I think exactly that, there's this
focusing on great power competition.

00:31:45.299 --> 00:31:50.210
Do you think there is a risk of overlooking
things like, let's say, climate change or

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:53.360
even problems that are building in other areas
of the world?

00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:58.751
STARES: I think there's definitely a risk
that there's such bipartisan pressure to be

00:31:58.751 --> 00:32:04.070
tough on Russia and China and we know why,
we understand why that is.

00:32:04.070 --> 00:32:09.929
No one can excuse what Russia's done in Ukraine
or the intimidating aggressive tactics of

00:32:09.929 --> 00:32:11.450
Beijing.

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:17.670
But at the same time, we've got to figure
out a way to, you either compartmentalize

00:32:17.670 --> 00:32:24.510
things, you come to some understanding that
there are certain existential problems, life-or-death

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:30.450
issues that we have to deal with and we're
going to put the other stuff to the side and

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:32.399
focus on this.

00:32:32.399 --> 00:32:38.020
And to some people, the need to do this is
so strong that it will happen.

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:44.880
I'm less optimistic because human nature can
be frankly self-defeating and irrational.

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:50.940
And so, I'm not totally sure that we will
strike that bargain and separate these things.

00:32:50.940 --> 00:32:53.309
So I hope I'm wrong, but-

00:32:53.309 --> 00:32:54.309
SIERRA: Yes.

00:32:54.309 --> 00:32:55.740
Break the streak.

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:59.450
STARES: Human nature is not always so rational
and benign, so-

00:32:59.450 --> 00:33:03.899
SIERRA: Well, hopefully when we speak to you
next season, everything on your list will

00:33:03.899 --> 00:33:10.500
be amiss and if not, at least we're better
prepared for what's to come this year.

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:11.500
STARES: Amen.

00:33:11.500 --> 00:33:13.159
SIERRA: Thank you for joining us, Paul.

00:33:13.159 --> 00:33:15.030
STARES: You're very welcome.

00:33:15.030 --> 00:33:21.070
SIERRA: Forecasting is an inexact science,
but it’s also a necessary one, especially

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:23.649
when it comes to international relations.

00:33:23.649 --> 00:33:27.100
It pays to be aware, and it pays to have a
plan.

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:31.669
But the PPS is only a snapshot of the risks
as they exist now.

00:33:31.669 --> 00:33:35.510
And if there's one thing we've learned in
recent years, it's that global conditions

00:33:35.510 --> 00:33:37.570
can shift rapidly.

00:33:37.570 --> 00:33:48.380
Whatever happens, we'll be here, watching
